# Engineering plan review (this repo only)

This rule lives in the EnglishApp project `.cursor/rules/` and applies only when this workspace is open—not to other projects.

When producing a **Plan** (Plan mode / `CreatePlan` / step-by-step implementation outline) that will **modify code** (app, web, SQL/migrations, scripts, configs that change runtime behavior), act as a **senior software engineer** first—plans must be technically rigorous, not a task shopping list.

## When to apply

Trigger **before** writing or presenting the plan whenever the work will touch compilable sources, schema, APIs, client/server contracts, or behavior-affecting config.

## When to skip

Skip the full SWE checklist when the plan is **only**:

- Docs / changelog / Cursor rules with no code or schema change
- Pure ops runbooks with no repo edits
- User explicitly skips（「跳过工程评审」「直接列步骤」等）—then do a minimal confirmation and proceed
- Same conversation already completed this review for an unchanged technical scope

If a **PM feature review** also applies, run PM first (product direction), then this SWE review (technical plan)—do not collapse them into vague “do the thing” bullets.

## Required checklist (cover in order; keep short, do not skip items)

1. **Problem & constraints** — Concrete failure/need; non-goals; hard constraints (platform, RLS, offline, perf, existing APIs)
2. **Current system** — Relevant modules, data flow, schema/contracts; cite real paths after a quick repo check (`docs/`, code, migrations)—no guessing architecture
3. **Design options** — At least 2 approaches when non-trivial; pick one with a one-line rationale (complexity, risk, fit with Android/web/Supabase stack)
4. **Change surface** — Files/layers touched; API/schema/permission impact; migrations & backward compatibility; what stays unchanged
5. **Correctness & edges** — Failure modes, empty/error states, concurrency/idempotency, authz; how to verify (compile, tests, smoke queries)
6. **Sequencing** — Ordered steps with dependencies; smallest shippable slice first; rollback or safe feature-flag notes when risky
7. **Open questions** — Ask 1–2 blocking technical questions first; state assumptions for the rest, then `CreatePlan`

## Output shape

- Lead with a short **工程审视** (decisions + why), then the plan steps—do not dump file lists without rationale
- Plans must name **concrete paths, contracts, and verification**—avoid “改一下相关代码” / “适当重构”
- Prefer the **smallest correct change**; call out scope creep explicitly if the user asked for more than needed
- After implementation, still follow compile / docs / remote Supabase rules; this rule does **not** require a `docs/` write for the review alone

## Anti-patterns

- Vague steps (“update the backend”, “handle edge cases”) with no module or contract
- Ignoring existing patterns in-repo in favor of a greenfield redesign
- Skipping schema/RLS/permission impact when data or roles are involved
- Over-engineering (extra layers, premature abstractions) without a stated need
- Treating the plan as a PM feature brief—product “what” belongs in PM review; this rule owns technical “how” and “why this how”
